    pageOne: [
      "A map is a model of a place, not a tiny copy containing everything. A road map emphasizes routes, while a weather map may show rainfall or temperature. A park trail map highlights paths and landmarks. Each map tells a story by selecting information that serves a particular purpose.",
      "A title identifies the map’s subject. A legend, sometimes called a key, explains symbols and colours. A scale compares distance on the map with distance in the real world. A north arrow or compass rose helps readers understand direction, although some maps use a different orientation and label it clearly.",
      "Symbols make complex information fit into limited space. A blue line might represent a river, and a star might mark a capital. Symbols do not have one universal meaning, so readers should check the legend instead of assuming. The same colour can represent elevation, land use, or a bus route on different maps.",
      "Scale affects the amount of detail. A large-scale neighbourhood map can show individual streets and buildings across a small area. A small-scale world map covers far more area but cannot label every road. Choosing a scale means deciding which details will remain useful and readable.",
      "Every flat world map also uses a projection, a method for representing Earth’s curved surface on a plane. Any projection changes some combination of shape, area, distance, or direction. Mapmakers select projections suited to their goals rather than finding one perfectly distortion-free world map.",
    ],
    pageOneCallout: { label: "Map Reader Checklist", text: "Read the title, legend, scale, direction marker, date, and source." },
    pageTwoSubheading: "Reading the Choices Behind a Map",
    pageTwo: [
      "Maps can also show change over time. A sequence might trace a town’s growth, an animal’s migration, or a storm’s path. Dates matter because roads, boundaries, coastlines, and measured conditions can change. An accurate old map may still be unsuitable for a present-day trip.",
      "Modern map data can come from ground surveys, satellites, aerial photographs, sensors, and public records. Geographic information systems, or GIS, help people store and compare layers. A planner might examine roads, slopes, streams, and population as separate layers before making a decision.",
      "Mapmakers make judgments about categories and labels. A map of ‘green space’ must define whether school fields, gardens, forests, and vacant lots belong together. Clear notes and sources let readers understand those decisions. A polished map can still mislead if its date, scale, categories, or source is hidden.",
      "To compare two maps, begin with purpose rather than asking which one is simply better. A subway diagram may change physical distances so routes are easy to follow. A topographic map uses contour lines to represent elevation. Each can be accurate for its intended task while looking very different.",
      "Maps tell stories through what they include, emphasize, and leave out. Careful readers use map features to interpret that story, then ask who made the map, when, from which data, and for what purpose. These questions turn a colourful picture into evidence that can be interpreted thoughtfully.",
    ],
    pageTwoCallout: { label: "Compare Fairly", text: "Judge a map by whether its choices support its stated purpose." },
