      "During spring and summer, many leaves appear green because they contain plenty of chlorophyll. This pigment helps plants capture light energy used to make sugars through photosynthesis. Leaves also contain other pigments, but the strong green colour often hides their yellow and orange shades from our view.",
      "As autumn approaches in places with distinct seasons, days become shorter and temperatures change. Many deciduous trees respond by slowing and eventually stopping chlorophyll production in their leaves. Existing chlorophyll breaks down, so its green colour fades instead of being continually replaced.",
      "When green fades, yellow and orange pigments called carotenoids become easier to see. These pigments were present in the leaf during summer; they did not suddenly arrive with cool weather. Carotenoids also give colour to foods such as carrots and corn, although leaves contain many different pigment mixtures.",
      "Some trees develop red or purple colours from pigments called anthocyanins. These can form in leaves during autumn when sugars become trapped and conditions are suitable. Weather influences the intensity of colour. Sunny days and cool nights above freezing may support bright red displays in some species.",
      "No single rule predicts every tree. Species, local weather, soil moisture, and location all affect timing and colour. An oak may hold rusty leaves while a nearby maple becomes red and a birch turns yellow. A tree’s changing palette reflects both its biology and its environment.",
