    skillTags: ["sequence", "cause and effect", "main idea", "life science"],
    pageOneSubheading: "A Dusting of Pollen",
    pageOne: [
      "A bee visiting a flower is usually looking for food. Many adult bees drink sugary nectar, and they collect protein-rich pollen to feed their young. As a bee reaches into a blossom, tiny pollen grains can cling to hairs on its body. This accidental dusting begins an important process.",
      "Flowers contain structures that help plants reproduce. Pollen is produced in a part called the anther. For pollination to occur, pollen must reach a receptive surface called the stigma of a flower of the same species. Wind can move pollen in some plants, but many flowering plants rely on animals.",
      "When a bee flies to another flower, some grains may brush from its body onto that flower’s stigma. If the pollen is compatible, it can lead to fertilization inside the flower. Seeds then begin to develop, often within a fruit. Pollination does not instantly create a ripe apple, but it starts a necessary chain.",
      "Flowers attract visitors in different ways. Colourful petals, patterns visible in ultraviolet light, shapes, and scents can guide pollinators. A blossom’s nectar may be deep inside a tube or easy to reach on a flat surface. Different features suit different visitors, including bees, butterflies, moths, birds, and bats.",
      "Bees are especially effective pollinators because they often visit many flowers of one species during a trip. This behaviour improves the chance that pollen reaches the correct kind of flower. Their branched body hairs can also carry grains efficiently as they move between blossoms.",
    ],
    pageOneCallout: { label: "Pollination Path", text: "Anther → bee’s body → stigma of another flower of the same species." },
    pageTwoSubheading: "Supporting Busy Pollinators",
    pageTwo: [
      "Pollinators help both wild plant communities and farms. Many fruits, nuts, and vegetables depend at least partly on animal pollination. The exact need differs among crops, and grasses such as wheat and corn are mainly wind-pollinated. Still, pollinators contribute to a varied food supply and to seeds eaten by wildlife.",
      "Bees include thousands of species, and not all live in large hives. Many native bees are solitary. A female may build a small nest in bare ground or a hollow stem. These bees do not make jars of honey, yet they can be valuable local pollinators.",
      "A pollinator-friendly garden offers flowers across the growing season. Groups of native flowering plants can provide nectar and pollen while fitting local conditions. Avoiding unnecessary pesticide use helps reduce risk to visiting insects. Gardeners can also leave some safe nesting spaces, such as undisturbed soil or hollow stems.",
      "People should observe bees calmly and give them space. A feeding bee is busy gathering resources, not performing for an audience. Never disturb a nest, and ask a knowledgeable adult for help if one appears near a busy doorway or another unsafe location.",
      "The next time a bee lands on a flower, notice the partnership. The bee gains food, while the plant gains a chance for pollen transfer. A single visit may look small, but repeated visits connect blossoms, support seed production, and help many ecosystems continue their seasonal cycles.",
    ],
